Blob Store and Sync

The blob store holds file bytes and image layers. Specs carry digests only,
so the journal stays pointers-only at any size. See docs/files for use from
CLI, SDK, and reconciler, and docs/architecture for registry placement in
data flow.

Store shape

Blobs split into 256KB chunks. Each chunk hashes with sha256; the root is
sha256 over the joined chunk hex digests. A manifest binds version,
chunkSize, totalSize, ordered chunk list, and root. Readers stream one
verified chunk at a time, so memory stays flat.

Limits: raw in-memory reads cap at 32MB, manifests cap at 1MB, chunked blobs
scale to 32GB on the wire. The registry keeps a 10MB manifest cap for OCI
compat; sync enforces 1MB for chunked manifests.

Two repos, one namespace

File artifacts use concord-files on the wire (monolithic OCI for compat).
Chunk sync state uses concord-blobs (one tag per replicated root as the peer
publish index). Serving is repo-independent: the registry answers a content
digest from its blob namespace no matter which repo pushed it, so one
canonical fetch prefix reaches every blob on the peer.

Wire verbs

HEAD and GET blobs, POST uploads with digest query, PUT and GET manifests,
GET tags list. Status codes follow distribution conventions: 200 on hit, 201
on upload or manifest put, 400 on bad digest or tag, 404 on unknown blob or
manifest, 413 over size caps.

Sync tick

Anti-entropy runs every 5s, mirroring the journal pull cadence
(internal/peersync blobPullInterval). Each tick tries wanted digests first,
then peer-published tags, against alive peers in rotated order. Worker starts
stagger by 100ms slots plus 0-200ms jitter, with at most 4 concurrent fetches
and 8 roots per tick, so one far-behind peer cannot starve the loop.

Two shapes replicate. Chunked roots arrive as manifest plus chunks. Raw
content digests (file bytes, image layers) arrive as one stream, split
locally into fixed pieces, and publish as an alias manifest so later ticks
use the resumable chunked path. Each digest comes from the first peer that
serves it; there is no all to all exchange.

Cursors

Blob cursors record next chunk index per peer and root in bbolt
(blobcursors bucket, memory mirror for tests). Saves only advance, never
retreat, so restarts and partitions resume mid-blob instead of refetching
from chunk zero. A corrupt entry self-heals to zero and retries.

Blocking rule

A missing blob blocks its workload by design. Ensure reports an error until
every wanted digest is complete, and the reconciler must not start before
Ensure returns nil. The miss path fails the pull, aborts the start, and
retries next tick, with Ensure as a fast-path nudge over the background tick.
A digest that never arrives blocks that workload indefinitely on a partition:
half-provisioned is worse than waiting.

Depot doctrine

Treat bulk bytes as freight, not signal. Stage large models at the depot
anchor over wire before the mission, pre-warm nodes while charging so
rollouts cost nothing, stagger version rollouts across groups sharing one
cell, and measure a full-size push over the real link before changing
protocol (see docs/deployment).

Closed durables and bind

There are exactly four durable things: journal, kv store (bbolt views plus
sync cursors), blob store (chunks, content-addressed), track file. Clients
use 127.0.0.1 loopback on registry port 8444; the registry binds 0.0.0.0 on
the same port so peers sync over the mesh with no second listener.

Caps

  chunk size: 256KB (blob.ChunkSize)
  manifest JSON sync and store readback: 1MB
  registry manifest compat: 10MB
  raw in-memory (GetRaw, MaxRawGet): 32MB
  streaming wire fetch and blob cap: 32GB
  concurrent root fetches per tick: 4
  roots per tick: 8
  tick: 5s, stagger 100ms plus 0-200ms jitter
